\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Finite identities for the binary tetrahedral spinor}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{Let #{2T} be the group of 24 Hurwitz units in the
[binary tetrahedral quotient](fdip-007G). In this model the
central group element is the quaternion #{-1}. The
[distinguished central involution](fgap-0014) of the abstract
group is the preimage of that quaternion under the comparison
isomorphism, not the quaternion itself. Let
#{\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)} be the conjugation homomorphism of
\ref{fdip-007G}, and write #{R_T=\pi(2T)}. View #{\mathbb H}
as a right vector space over
#{\mathbb C=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{1,i\}}. Left
multiplication is complex-linear on that right-#{\mathbb C}
spinor; write #{\rho_s} for this representation. Its
realification #{\rho_s^{\mathbb R}} acts on the underlying
real space #{\mathbb H_R}. Let #{\tau} be the representation
on #{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{i,j,k\}}
given by #{\tau(q)v=qvq^{-1}}. Both are the fibers of
[the two-fiber example](fdip-007K). The substrate action
#{\alpha} is trivial. Realify that right-#{\mathbb C} spinor
to #{\mathbb H_R} before the sum, and set
##{F=\mathbb H_R\oplus\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H,\qquad
\rho=\rho_s^{\mathbb R}\oplus\tau.}
The selector #{\sigma} of the
[compatibility datum](fdip-009Z) is the idempotent which is
the identity on #{\mathbb H_R} and zero on
#{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}. The abstract involution #{z}
maps to the quaternion #{-1}, so this selector is the image of
the central idempotent #{e_-=(1_A-[z])/2} under #{\rho}. That
idempotent is the one in [the real group algebra](fgap-0015).
The reference comparison #{C} is the pair of homomorphisms
#{\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)} and the vertex identification
#{R_T\cong A_4} proved below.}

\p{Then #{\ker(\pi|_{2T})=\{1,-1\}}, the vertex action induces
#{R_T\cong A_4}, and the selector is
##{P=I_{\mathbb H_R}\oplus 0.}
Left multiplication by the quaternion #{-1} is #{-1} on the
quaternion module, so that module is fixed by #{e_-} and
annihilated by #{e_+=(1_A+[z])/2}. The products of the #{d=3}
Clifford operators
of [the spatial Dirac operator](fdip-000R) reproduce the
quaternion multiplication table. None of these identities
supplies a covariance law for that Dirac operator.}

\p{Remark. For the right-#{\mathbb C} structure on #{\mathbb H},
the ordered basis of the matrix map #{M} is #{(1,-j)}.
\ref{fdip-00M6} records the matrix of left multiplication by
#{a+bi+cj+dk} in that basis,
##{M(a+bi+cj+dk)=
 \begin{pmatrix}a+bi&c+di\\-c+di&a-bi\end{pmatrix}.}}

\proof{
\p{The kernel statement is the restriction of
#{\ker\pi=\{1,-1\}} from \ref{fdip-007G}. The same source
identifies #{2T} with the full preimage #{\pi^{-1}(R_T)} and
gives #{|R_T|=12}. The group #{2T} is the 24 Hurwitz units, so
#{\ker(\pi|_{2T})=\{1,-1\}}. Substituting the quaternion #{-1}
into the matrix of the Remark gives #{M(-1)=-I_2}, so
#{\rho_s(-1)=-I_2}. Conjugation by the quaternion #{-1} is
the identity, so #{\tau(-1)=I_3}. The abstract involution
maps to that quaternion, so #{\rho(-1)=\rho(z)}. Realification
carries #{\rho_s(-1)} to #{-I} on #{\mathbb H_R}. Therefore
##{\rho(-1)=\rho(z)=-I_{\mathbb H_R}\oplus I_{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H},}
and the selector in the statement satisfies
##{P=\frac{I-\rho(-1)}2=I_{\mathbb H_R}\oplus 0.}
On the quaternion module, left multiplication by the quaternion
#{-1} is #{-1}, so that module's algebra action sends #{e_-}
to #{1} and #{e_+} to #{0}. The abstract involution maps to
that quaternion, and #{\rho=\rho_s^{\mathbb R}\oplus\tau}, so
##{\rho(e_-)=(I-\rho(z))/2=P,}
while
##{\rho(e_+)=(I+\rho(z))/2=0\oplus I_{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}.}
The scalar #{0} is the quaternion-module action of #{e_+},
not this operator.}

\p{Declare the ordered basis #{(i,j,k)} of
#{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}. On the unreified fibers, in
the ordered basis #{(1,-j)} and this basis,
#{\rho_s(-1)\oplus\tau(-1)=-I_2\oplus I_3}. The same
difference of blocks is the complex block form
##{P=I_2\oplus 0,\qquad
\frac{I-(-I_2\oplus I_3)}2=I_2\oplus 0,}
and the chart of #{e_+} is #{0\oplus I_3}. Realification
replaces the spinor block #{-I_2} by #{-I} on #{\mathbb H_R};
the statement uses that realified sum. Label the 4 imaginary
vectors of \ref{fdip-007G} by
##{v_1=(1,1,1),\; v_2=(1,-1,-1),\;
v_3=(-1,1,-1),\; v_4=(-1,-1,1).}
Their differences
##{v_2-v_1=(0,-2,-2),\; v_3-v_1=(-2,0,-2),\;
v_4-v_1=(-2,-2,0)}
are linearly independent: after removing the common factor
#{-2}, the determinant of
##{\begin{pmatrix}0&1&1\\1&0&1\\1&1&0\end{pmatrix}}
equals #{2}. A rotation fixing all 4 vertices therefore
fixes a basis of #{\mathbb R^3} and is the identity. The
vertex action gives an injective homomorphism
#{R_T\to S_4}.}

\p{Conjugation by #{i} is the coordinate map
#{(x,y,z)\mapsto(x,-y,-z)}. Indeed, for
#{v=xi+yj+zk},
##{iv=(-x)+(yk-zj),\qquad
(iv)(-i)=xi-yj-zk,}
where the last step uses #{ji=-k} and #{ki=j}. The same
expansion with cycled units gives
##{j:(x,y,z)\mapsto(-x,y,-z),\qquad
k:(x,y,z)\mapsto(-x,-y,z).}
On the 4 vertices these are the permutations
##{(1\;2)(3\;4),\qquad(1\;3)(2\;4),\qquad(1\;4)(2\;3).}
Each is even. Let #{u=-1+i+j+k} and
#{\omega=u/2\in 2T}. Direct multiplication yields
##{\begin{array}{c|c}
v&uv\overline u/4\\\hline
v_1&v_1\\
v_2&v_4\\
v_3&v_2\\
v_4&v_3
\end{array}}
so #{\pi(\omega)} fixes #{v_1} and acts by the cycle
#{(2\;4\;3)}. This cycle is even. Conjugation by it permutes
the three double transpositions:
##{\begin{aligned}
(2\;4\;3)(1\;2)(3\;4)(2\;4\;3)^{-1}&=(1\;4)(2\;3),\\
(2\;4\;3)(1\;4)(2\;3)(2\;4\;3)^{-1}&=(1\;3)(2\;4),\\
(2\;4\;3)(1\;3)(2\;4)(2\;4\;3)^{-1}&=(1\;2)(3\;4).
\end{aligned}}
The cycle has order #{3}, while every non-identity element of
that Klein subgroup has order #{2}, so the two subgroups meet
only at the identity. These 12 permutations are the vertex
permutations of products in #{R_T}. They are distinct, and
#{|R_T|=12}, so they exhaust the vertex image. All 12 are
even. Combined with injectivity, the vertex map is an
isomorphism #{R_T\cong A_4}. The composite
#{2T\to R_T\to A_4} has kernel #{\{1,-1\}}.}

\p{For the #{d=3} Clifford operators of \ref{fdip-000R},
#{\gamma_a^2=I} and
#{\gamma_a\gamma_b=-\gamma_b\gamma_a} whenever #{a\ne b}.
Set
##{I\mapsto 1,\quad
\gamma_1\gamma_2\mapsto i,\quad
\gamma_2\gamma_3\mapsto j,\quad
\gamma_1\gamma_3\mapsto k.}
Then
##{(\gamma_1\gamma_2)^2=-\gamma_1^2\gamma_2^2=-I,}
and the same sign occurs for the other two products. Also
##{(\gamma_1\gamma_2)(\gamma_2\gamma_3)=\gamma_1\gamma_3,}
and the two cyclic products match #{jk=i} and #{ki=j}.
These are the quaternion relations. They identify an action
of the same abstract group on the even products. They do not
state an intertwiner between left multiplication and the
spatial operator #{\sum_j\gamma_j\nabla_j}.}
}
